 creative ways to allow  you to  move things between let's say your  inventory and your belt or your  inventory and your stash and shortcuts  and  the way that it feels in terms of like  some skills are dependent like  sorcerer's teleport you know it depends  on where you click to  and so now we're having to sort of  predict you know a certain range of  distance and those sorts of things based  on  how you use the controller but it  fundamentally is just a change from  you're telling the you know the  character where to go to you are the  character  giving them you know that direction  through the sticks and  that was the the team um you know the  dev team just did a fantastic job it's  like  the very first time we implemented  controller support it was already very  playable and it felt  very it felt great to play as especially  as like i said  i'm similar to you like i play d3 on  gamepad mostly  and so i was kind of interested to be  like how does this gonna feel  to control because it's not built from  the ground up for this and so how how is  it going to work and  it feels great like especially like  ranged attacks feel really good melee  it's actually really good it's just  stuff like why  you know auto gold pickup works so well  it's like one of the things going in and  trying to identify every single piece of  gold and picking them up  you know with every button press was  just too much for like and  so there's certain things that get  turned on as an option now those things  you can turn off  and that's a lot of things that we did  too is like when we made a choice we  sometimes made it an option where  maybe somebody loves auto gold pickup  and maybe somebody doesn't and so they  can choose whether to turn it on or turn  it off  based on their playstyle but yeah it was  really just trying to make it  feel more native you know even though  that it wasn't and i could you don't  want it to feel like oh this is  a pc port and therefore it feels kind of  like  i don't know clunky and those sorts of  things and so we've dedicated a lot of  effort in trying to make that feel  smooth  and to feel like you have precision and  feel like you can do what you want to do  and it feels like a great console  experience moving to controller had it  uh any effect on how you perceive the  balance of certain classes  if at all no i mean it all i mean like i  said there are certain things where  you would have a certain precision about  where you could do  certain things but the same thing for  diablo 3 is that certain builds like  certain barbarian builds are easier with  mouse and keyboard than they are with  controller as an example so  one of the things we really because were  trying to deliver that authentic  experience we just didn't want to mess  with balance  that you know we know that there are  certain balance issues and potentially  like the  you know people call the hammered in  with the paladin with um  is is a strong character build currently  in the building but  that idea that we were changing a bunch  of things visually we didn't want to get  into changing the balance of how the  game  plays too because it felt a little too  risky and so  everything we did was very focused on  keeping the balance as it is  so this open beta that's coming up is  going to be the time you get to  kind of test out multiplayer because i  know technical alpha was uh  single-player only get our got our feet  wet there but  i'm kind of excited to jump back into d2  multiplayer i remember my friends back  in high school  doing a lot of they carried me often and  i expect them to do it again this time  around  um but yeah what can we expect from  the multiplayer aspects in the beta or  even the full game  yeah well i mean that's one of the part  of we're getting ready with september  23rd as our release date you know we're  going to do this august multiplayer beta  and it's really  you know you want to test out all the  systems we're going to this sort of  we're going to the battlenet you know  global battlenet servers and we're going  to have all these blizzard servers  running and we want to be able to see  okay let's put a large load on it let's  test our services let's test our servers  and get people playing that experience  they didn't get to play in the technical  alpha so  some great things that one is you know  obviously again the big difference is  multiplayer versus single player so the  beta is  unique for that and so you can either  bring people together and play co-op  up to eight people um or play pvp if you  want to  and then uh it's on console so this will  be the first time people get to  experience d2r on console which will be  a big moment  and uh and then we're expanding the  classes so we're allowing people to play  five classes instead of the three in  alpha so we've added the druid and the  paladin to the mix and so  again you can go in and play as a judo  paladin and and see how they play as  well  i've heard that you are a big fan of the  redone cinematics so  all of the cinematics have been like  revamped um  that's that's kind of why can you take  me behind the scenes in terms of  how the team did approach that or even  like just pulled it off  yeah i mean it was really around you  know when we look at the philosophy of  the game the philosophy is really about  it's a remaster not a remake like we  wanted  we're taking the purity and the  authenticity of the original and we want  to just  elevate it with you know with sort of 4k  art and the surround sound and such  and so but when we looked at the  cinematics like a lot of remasters will  take the cinematics and say oh let's  just upres them  or do something that because you don't  necessarily have the source material  anymore and you can't just kind of work  from what you had  years ago and we just felt like the when  you think about d2r  or d2 it it's such a genre defining game  and it had such a meaningful moment in  time 20 years ago that  we felt like it really deserved to have  the cinematics remade  and so we went into it going okay we  don't want to change how we tell the  story and we don't want to change  what the story is because people talked  about like oh could you shorten it or  could you give a different perspective  or could you and we're like no we want  to be  faithful to the source material but what  we want to do is bring it forward again  in a really  modern way and so we went in and worked  with we have a  story and franchise development team  called sfd and  blizzard that they're the ones who work  on all the amazing cinematics that  blizzard makes  and so we partnered with them and we  were able to work  with another partner called access i  don't know if you know of them but they  we i worked with them on gears of war as  well as part of our hive buster trailer  but they all came together and we're  going to remake this  shot for shot uh in the highest cg that  we can do in the time that we have and  make and  you get a taste of that in the trailer  you get to see little snippets of it and  i can't wait for people to see more  because it's everyone did a phenomenal  job you know like that  again i feel like it elevates d2r  because  the quality of the storytelling now that  you get all that detail  that you maybe didn't see before the the  were those beetles on the floor were  there spiders on the floor like no  they're beetles you know they're beetles  like i can see that detail and i can see  you know all the different things that  are happening and sort of the story  telling what's going on in the  environment and on the characters and  their faces  and their emotions that they're having  and so it's i love it and i think it  i think it really takes it to another  place i have one more question  and it's kind of a kind of a broad one  to hit to you but  uh you've moved to a blizzard fairly  recently and  it's still kind of wild to think that  you're that you are representing diablo  um now but what have been the biggest  changes going from  you know shaping gears of war and now  shaping diablo like  how you work what you work on the things  you focus on in game development how you  handle your teams what has changed for  you man  i think the the number one thing has got  to be the pandemic the  i only had eight days in office when i  first arrived and then we're all working  from home and where i'm  i'm used to being you know walking the  halls and standing up  in front of the team and and working  with people uh one-on-one and such  that that everything is through you know  a video camera now and uh  it's it's been surreal to do that and  i've i mean i've been with them now  15 months and it's like like i said  except for eight days it's all been  through  uh through a camera and so it's really  changed how i try to lead it changes how  i  communicate it changes a lot of things  and so it's also been interesting  because we're  with gears you know i i joined gears  back in 2005 and i was basically there  pretty much the whole way and i was kind  of the  the person who was the keeper of the  lore and the keeper of the franchise i  was the creative director of it  and so i was the one teaching and and  others were learning  and now i'm coming into a franchise that  i've loved you know  since the original d1 and and having  played it  for all this time but still you're  coming i'm coming in as a student that  i don't know all the lore and i don't  know every secret and everywhere every  skeleton is kind of  in their closet around you know like how  the game works in the way that certain  details about certain systems and  so i've had to come in much more as a as  a student of of  the game than i was when i was the head  of the coalition sort of running gears  of war so  that's been really interesting it's part  of what and you know it's what helped  inspire me to do this was just  it's a new challenge uh getting to work  on one of my favorite franchises  and getting to work with the caliber of  tell him i get to work out at blizzard  so  uh it's been interesting like i said  doing it  you know from my dining room in north  carolina as a strange place to be  to be trying to lead three diablo games  to ship but um  but it's been uh it's been exciting i  appreciate the time  that you gave to us to talk about diablo  and your work and your career  is there anything else that you want to  share before i let you go here well i  think the one thing that  i really want to call out too around the  beta is just that the pre-order is  happening so that's one of the big  things is that with our announcing our  ship date  uh at the e3 event we can now take  pre-orders for console  in terms of uh and what that also those  pre-orders allows you to get into the  open beta  sooner so we're actually going to do the  beta in two phases and so there'll be a  pre-order access phase and then an open  phase so i highly recommend and we're  also going to we have a really cool  if you pre-order the game you'll  actually get a transmog  in d3 that will allow you to change your  barbarian both male and female to look  like the diablo ii barbarian  which i think is really kind of cool to  have that iconic barbarian look in d3  um so that's coming and like i said and  getting access to the pre-order beta uh  would be a great time to get in and like  i said get to play the five classes get  to see the game  and uh get you have to play early with  your friends rod ferguson thank you so  much for your time man take care  uh and uh yeah diablo 2 resurrected it's  coming soon  uh y'all should peep that too and um  yeah thank you all for watching this  segment here on diablo 2 resurrected  i want you all to stick to gamespot.com  because we have so many more interviews  gameplay reveals trailers and live  streams because we are raising money for  ablegamers  and i hope to see you all around next  [Music]  time  [Music] 